  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nToday-Monday: Hot and Humid with Storm Chances\n\nAfter a break from the hot and humid conditions, they are returning \ntoday and lingering through Monday. A ridge that has been sitting \noff to our west has begun to build eastward towards the Upper \nMidwest. This amplification of the ridge brings warmer and humid \nconditions for the next few days. The 850mb temperatures increase \ntoday and furthermore on Sunday with temperatures ranging from 24 to \n26C based on the mean HREF 850mb temperatures. Despite the cloud \ncover from this morning, temperatures will get into the mid to upper \n80s for most locations with some areas getting into the low 90s. The \nareas that see the warmest temperatures, basically our western tier \nof counties and Wabasha and Olmsted counties are in a Heat Advisory \nfor today as heat indices should get near 100F and up to 105F. Most \nother locations across the forecast area will see heat indices in \nthe upper 80s to upper 90s.\n\nFocusing on Sunday, there is also a decent EFI signal where values \nof 0.7 to 0.9 exist indicating that the forecasted temperatures are \non the warmer side of climatological normal. Dewpoints, aided by the \ncorn sweat, are expected to get into the mid and upper 70s for most \nareas. Portions of northeast Iowa and southeast Minnesota could see \ndewpoints in the low 80s. Combining both of these we get heat \nindices in the 100 to 115F range! The areas that have the best \nchances at seeing the 110F+ heat indices are more focused in \nnortheast Iowa and along the Mississippi River Valley. Poor \novernight recovery into Monday is possible as apparent temperatures \nstay in the mid to upper 70s for most locations. Uncertainty remains \nfor how hot the area gets on Monday due to potential convection. \nThis all being said, there is an Extreme Heat Warning for most of \nthe CWA for Sunday with a Heat Advisory for Clark, Taylor, Juneau, \nand Adams counties. As the ridge gradually shifts back south, a \nfront comes through the Upper Midwest. The timing of this front will \nplay a role in how hot we truly get on Monday. Current forecasted \nheat indices are in the low 100s for areas south of I-90 and in the \nmid to upper 90s to the north. If the front is slower to pass \nthrough, then the higher temperatures and dewpoints will be around \nlonger, helping to reach heat headline criteria. Due to the \nuncertainty for Monday regarding the timing of this front and the \npotential convection or convective debris, have kept the Extreme \nHeat Watch in place for northeast Iowa and far southwest Wisconsin. \n\nAs we turn our attention to the thunderstorm chances over the next \nfew days, they sure are difficult to forecast! Within this pattern, \nthe low-level shear is not impressive (mainly in the 10 to 25 kts \nrange for 0-1km shear), while the deep layer shear has some modest \nvalues to it (around 40 to 60 kts of 0-6km shear). Instability wise, \nthere is plenty of it, 2500 to 4000 J/kg of MLCAPE. There is also \nquite a few perturbations that pass through the Upper Midwest that \ncould be helpful for storm development. CAMs have come into good \nagreement that an MCS is expected to form in South Dakota this \nevening and make its way eastward along the theta-e gradient. \nEarlier runs of some of the CAMs brought this MCS all the way to our \nwestern half of the CWA, however only a couple CAMs remain that \ncontinue to support that. By Sunday, as mentioned previously, a few \nwaves move through the area increasing convective potential, however \nthere is low confidence on exact locations of storms for the \nafternoon and early evening. Instability and shear will be present \nall day, depending on morning convective debris the destabilization \ncould be delayed. CAMs have shown scattered storm development across \nthe entire CWA with each model showing different locations. With \nstrong northwest flow, 40 to 50 kts in the midlevels, these storms \nwould race southwestward with a damaging wind or hail risk. The main \nproblem for Sunday is if the capping in place will be overcome by \nthe weak waves that move through. If this occurs then there could be \nquite a few storms out there. Then the attention would turn to \noutflow boundaries and what those could do as they interact with \nstorms. Confidence does increase for the later evening as a slightly \nmore defined wave pushes into Wisconsin. This would increase storm \nchances for areas east of the Mississippi River. By Monday, a front \nmoves through the region, however there does not appear to be good \nconvergence along the front and combining this with the capping in \nplace, storms are not expected to fire for Monday afternoon and \nevening.\n\nTuesday-Saturday: Warm Temperatures Continue with Storm Chances\n\nThe ridge shifts westward just enough and northwest flow remains in \nplace. Both of these will mean temperatures cool down slightly, into \nthe mid to upper 80s but it also means that the 70F dewpoints would \nbe less likely to occur.",
